  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 149 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nUpper analysis shows a large ridge of high pressure over the west-\ncentral US with northwest flow over the Midwest states. At the \nsurface, a stationary/warm front is positioned from roughly \nBurlington IA to Flora IL. Isentropic upglide on the ~300K surface \nhas resulted in a consistent low stratus deck near/east of the \nboundary, ultimately holding temperatures down several degrees lower \nthan originally anticipated. Clearer skies west of the front have \nallowed temperatures to surge into the upper 80s to low 90s, paired \nwith dewpoints in the low 80s. Heat indices in these same areas have \nclimbed above 100 degrees with Springfield and Jacksonville IL \nreporting heat indices over 110 degrees as of 1 pm. Although cloud \ncover looks to be slow to depart, any pockets of clearing \nshould allow temperatures to quickly rebound. Because of this, \nno changes to our current heat products have been made.\n\nThings remain hot and humid on Monday as the 850 mb ridge axis moves \noverhead, keeping 22-25C 850 mb temperatures largely in place. \nMonday has the potential to be the hottest day of the forecast \nperiod, pending any thunderstorm activity/convective debris \nclouds. Multiple thunderstorm complexes could impact parts of \nthe region through Monday night as several upper shortwaves ride\nthe eastern periphery of the upper ridge and a cold front \napproaches from the north. CAMs continue to struggle with \nthunderstorm placement and evolution, which has led to \ndifficulty pinpointing timing. Water vapor imagery shows a \nsubtle shortwave disturbance over eastern South Dakota/southern \nMinnesota with composite radar showing some storms ahead of that\nwave, potentially serving as our first opportunity for \nconvective activity later this afternoon or evening. Additional\nwaves are expected to track through the Midwest states \novernight into Monday and could impact parts of the area. Any \nstorms that do develop will have the potential to become severe \nas they move through an environment characterized by very strong\ninstability (4000+ J/kg SBCAPE) and seasonally strong wind \nshear (30- 40 kts). Hazards with any severe storms would be \ndamaging wind gusts in excess of 60 mph and torrential rainfall.\n\nTemperatures and dewpoints drop closer to seasonal normals on \nTuesday as the airmass modifies behind a cold front. Northwest flow \nwill remain in place aloft through the upcoming week and may open \nthe door for shortwave disturbances through next weekend. A brief \nwindow of surface high pressure should shield precipitation chances \nthrough midweek before a stronger upper wave approaches later in the \nweek.",
